connector/cn_proc: an exec's event carries its command line

An exec's event names the process by its PIDs alone. A listener that wants
its command line reads /proc/PID/cmdline, and a short-lived process has
often ended by then: its mm is gone once it exits, and its /proc once it
is reaped. Of 201 processes a shell in a shards guest started in a loop
(/bin/true, 200 times), a listener recorded 148 to 159, and 33 to 38 of
those without a command line (shards, docs/research/platform-measurements.md
M176).

Send the command line after the event: the arguments' whole length as a
__u32, then as many of their bytes as a page holds, NUL-terminated as
/proc/PID/cmdline gives them. The exec has just laid them out on the new
stack, and the new program has not run, so they are what the exec was
given, whatever the program writes over them later. Where there is no
memory for the longer message (GFP_NOWAIT, which does not warn), the event
goes as before, and its listener reads /proc as it would have.

A listener that reads the fixed-size event alone is unaffected: msg->len
says how much follows it.

